---
title: How to Follow Up on a Job Application (With Email Templates and Timelines)
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: job-seekers
---
**Quick answer:** Wait about one week after applying before you follow up, unless the job post gives a timeline. Send a short email or LinkedIn message to the recruiter or hiring manager: say which role you applied for and when, add one line about why you're a strong fit, and ask whether there's an update on the timeline. After an interview, send a thank-you within 24 hours and follow up once more if you haven't heard back by the date they gave you. Two follow-ups per stage is enough; after that, move on.

Silence after an application is the most frustrating part of job hunting. A good follow-up won't change a "no" into a "yes", but it can bring your application to the top of a busy recruiter's list, and it sometimes gets you a clear answer so you can stop waiting.

## Should you follow up on a job application?

Usually, yes, if you can reach a real person. Recruiters handle many roles at once, and a polite note can help. A follow-up is most useful when:

- You applied through a referral or know who the hiring manager is.
- The company is small or a startup, where founders often hire directly.
- It's been more than a week with no reply.
- You have new information, like another offer or a relevant result.

It matters less for very large companies where you applied through a portal and can't identify the recruiter.

## When to follow up: a timeline

{% table %}
- Stage
- When to follow up
- How many times
---
- After applying
- 5–7 working days
- Once, maybe twice
---
- After a phone screen
- Thank-you within 24 hours, follow up after the date they gave you
- Once
---
- After a final interview
- Thank-you within 24 hours, follow up 1–2 days after their deadline
- Once or twice
---
- After a verbal offer
- If the written offer hasn't arrived in 2–3 working days
- Once
---
- After a rejection
- Optional, within a week
- Once
{% /table %}

## How to find who to contact

- **The job post** sometimes names the recruiter or hiring manager.
- **LinkedIn**: search the company for "recruiter", "talent" or the team lead's title.
- **The company website**: at startups, the founder or CTO is often the hiring manager.
- **The confirmation email** after you applied may have a reply-to address.

Don't email five people at the same company. Pick one.

## Template 1: Following up after applying

**Subject:** Following up on my application for Backend Engineer

> Hi Priya,
>
> I applied for the Backend Engineer role on 3 November and wanted to follow up. I've spent the last three years building payment APIs in Go and Postgres, which lines up closely with the platform work described in the post.
>
> Is there any update on the hiring timeline? I'd be glad to share more about my experience.
>
> Thanks,
> Arjun Mehta
> [LinkedIn or portfolio link]

Keep it under 100 words. One clear reason you're a fit, one clear question.

## Template 2: Thank-you after an interview

**Subject:** Thank you – Frontend Engineer interview

> Hi Sam,
>
> Thanks for taking the time to speak with me today. I enjoyed hearing about the design system migration, and our conversation about moving components to server rendering made me even more interested in the role.
>
> Please let me know if there's anything else I can send. I'm looking forward to the next steps.
>
> Best,
> Lena

Mention one specific thing from the conversation. It shows you were listening and helps them remember you.

## Template 3: Following up after the interview deadline passes

**Subject:** Checking in – Frontend Engineer role

> Hi Sam,
>
> I hope your week is going well. You mentioned the team hoped to decide by Friday, so I wanted to check in on the timeline. I'm still very interested in the role.
>
> Thanks,
> Lena

## Template 4: When you have another offer

**Subject:** Timeline question – Data Engineer role

> Hi Marco,
>
> I wanted to let you know I've received an offer from another company and need to respond by 20 November. Your team is my first choice, so I wanted to ask whether there's any way to speed up the next step.
>
> Thanks for understanding,
> Aisha

Only send this if the offer is real. Many companies will move faster for a strong candidate.

## Template 5: After a rejection

**Subject:** Thank you – Product Engineer role

> Hi Jo,
>
> Thank you for letting me know, and for the time your team spent with me. I'd welcome any feedback you can share, and I'd love to be considered for future roles on the team.
>
> Best,
> Ravi

Not everyone gets feedback, but a gracious reply keeps the door open. People have been hired months later for a different role this way.

## What not to do

- **Don't follow up the day after applying.** Give them a week.
- **Don't send long emails.** Three to five sentences is plenty.
- **Don't guilt-trip.** "I haven't heard anything for two weeks, which is disappointing" won't help.
- **Don't call repeatedly** unless the post invites calls.
- **Don't follow up more than twice per stage.**
- **Don't attach your resume again** unless they ask.

## How to track your follow-ups

When you apply to many roles, it's easy to lose track of who you emailed and when. At minimum, keep a list of:

- Company and role
- Date applied
- Contact name
- Date of each follow-up
- Current status

The [GetHired agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/agent) keeps track of your applications, saved jobs and interviews in one chat, so you can ask "Which applications haven't moved in two weeks?" instead of digging through your inbox. Our guide to [using an AI job search agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-use-an-ai-job-search-agent-find-track-and-apply-to-jobs-from-one-chat) shows how.

## When to move on

If you've followed up twice and heard nothing, assume it's a no for now and focus your energy elsewhere. Silence is usually about a busy team, not about you. Keep applying in parallel so one role never holds up your whole search.

If you're getting no replies at all across many applications, the problem may be earlier in the process. Run your resume through an [ATS check](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes) and read [why resumes get ghosted](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/why-your-resume-is-getting-ghosted-and-how-semantic-ai-changes-everything).

## Frequently asked questions

### How long should I wait to follow up after applying?

About one week, or five to seven working days. If the job post says applications close on a certain date, wait until a few days after that.

### Is it better to follow up by email or LinkedIn?

Email is more professional if you have the address. LinkedIn works well when you don't, especially at startups. Keep the message just as short.

### Should I send a thank-you email after every interview?

Yes. Send it within 24 hours to the people you spoke with, or to the recruiter if you don't have their emails. It's quick and it's remembered.

### What if the recruiter said they'd be in touch and never was?

Follow up once a day or two after the date they gave you, and once more a week later. After that, move on.

### Can following up hurt my chances?

A polite, short follow-up almost never hurts. Repeated or pushy messages can.

## Next steps

1. Make a list of every application from the last two weeks.
2. Send one follow-up to the roles you care about most, using the templates above.
3. Keep applying: [browse new jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs) every few days so your pipeline stays full.
